1. locator()
Definition

locator() is the recommended modern way to find elements in Playwright.

It supports:

auto-waiting
retry mechanism
stable execution
Example
Locator loginBtn = page.locator("#login");

loginBtn.click();
Features

✅ Auto-wait
✅ Retry support
✅ Lazy evaluation
✅ Handles dynamic elements well
✅ Best practice in Playwright

Why Preferred?

Playwright resolves locator at action time.

If DOM changes:

locator still works properly.

Very useful for dynamic applications.

2. page.$()
Definition

page.$() returns the first matching ElementHandle immediately.

Example
ElementHandle element = page.$("#login");

element.click();
Problem with page.$()

❌ No auto-retry
❌ Less stable
❌ Can become stale
❌ Not recommended for modern usage

Important Interview Point

page.$() behaves more like Selenium WebElement.

If element changes:

handle may become invalid.
3. getByText()
Definition

Finds element using visible text.

Example
page.getByText("Login").click();
Best Used For
buttons
labels
visible UI validation
Benefits

✅ Readable tests
✅ User-centric approach
✅ Auto-wait included

Limitation

If multiple elements have same text:

ambiguity may happen.
4. getByRole()
Definition

Finds elements using ARIA roles and accessible names.

Example
page.getByRole(AriaRole.BUTTON,
    new Page.GetByRoleOptions().setName("Login"))
    .click();
Why Important?

This is Playwright’s most recommended locator strategy.

It improves:

accessibility testing
stable locators
maintainability
Common Roles
button
textbox
checkbox
link
heading
Why getByRole() is Powerful

It behaves like real user interaction.

Example:

user sees button
role identifies button semantically

Very stable against DOM changes.

1. Why is page.$() Discouraged in Playwright?

page.$() is discouraged because it returns an immediate static ElementHandle.

Example:

ElementHandle element = page.$("#login");

Problems:

❌ No auto-waiting
❌ No retry mechanism
❌ Can become stale if DOM changes
❌ Less reliable for dynamic applications
Real-Time Problem

Suppose:

React page re-renders button after API call.

Using:

ElementHandle btn = page.$("#submit");

After DOM refresh:

old handle becomes invalid
test may fail
Why Locator is Better
Locator btn = page.locator("#submit");
btn.click();

Locator finds fresh element during action execution.

This reduces flaky tests.

SDET2 Interview Answer

“page.$() is discouraged because it returns a static ElementHandle immediately without auto-waiting or retry support. In dynamic applications, the element may become stale after DOM updates, making tests flaky. Playwright recommends Locator APIs because they are more stable and resilient.”

2. What is Lazy Evaluation in Locator?
Definition

Locator does NOT immediately search for the element.

It searches only when action happens.

This is called lazy evaluation.

Example
Locator loginBtn = page.locator("#login");

At this point:
❌ element is NOT searched yet.

Only when:

loginBtn.click();

Playwright actually finds the element.

Why Important?

If DOM changes:

locator gets latest element state
avoids stale element problems
Selenium vs Playwright
Selenium
WebElement element = driver.findElement(By.id("login"));

Element found immediately.

Playwright
Locator locator = page.locator("#login");

Element resolved later during action.

SDET2 Interview Answer

“Lazy evaluation means Playwright locators do not immediately query the DOM when created. The actual element lookup happens only when an action or assertion is performed. This helps Playwright work reliably with dynamic DOM changes and reduces stale element issues.”

3. Which Locator Strategy is Most Stable?
Most Stable Strategy

✅ getByRole()

Example:

page.getByRole(AriaRole.BUTTON,
new Page.GetByRoleOptions().setName("Login"));
Why Most Stable?

Because it uses:

accessibility roles
user-visible behavior
semantic meaning

instead of fragile:

XPath
CSS structure
Stability Order (Generally)
getByRole()  → Most Stable
getByLabel()
getByText()
locator(css)
XPath → Least Stable
Why XPath is Less Stable?

DOM structure changes frequently.

Example:

//div[2]/span/button

Small UI changes can break test.

SDET2 Interview Answer

“getByRole() is considered the most stable locator strategy because it uses accessibility roles and user-facing attributes instead of DOM structure. It is resilient to UI layout changes and aligns with real user interactions.”

4. Difference Between ElementHandle and Locator
Feature	ElementHandle	Locator
Nature	Static reference	Dynamic query
Auto Wait	❌ No	✅ Yes
Retry Support	❌ No	✅ Yes
Lazy Evaluation	❌ No	✅ Yes
Dynamic DOM Handling	Poor	Excellent
Recommended	❌ No	✅ Yes
ElementHandle

Represents actual DOM element at a specific moment.

Example:

ElementHandle el = page.$("#login");

If DOM updates:

handle may become stale.
Locator

Represents logic to find element.

Example:

Locator locator = page.locator("#login");

Each action:

re-fetches latest element.
Most Important Interview Point
Locator is “smart”
ElementHandle is “fixed”
SDET2 Interview Answer

“ElementHandle is a static reference to a DOM element captured at a specific point in time, whereas Locator is a dynamic abstraction that resolves the element during actions and assertions. Locator supports auto-waiting, retries, and lazy evaluation, making it more reliable for dynamic applications.”

5. Why Does Playwright Recommend getByRole()?

Playwright recommends getByRole() because it:

mimics real user behavior
improves accessibility testing
creates stable locators
reduces flaky tests
Example
page.getByRole(AriaRole.BUTTON,
new Page.GetByRoleOptions().setName("Submit"));
Benefits
1. Accessibility Friendly

Uses:

ARIA roles
accessible names

Encourages accessible UI development.

2. More Stable

Independent of:

CSS classes
DOM hierarchy
3. Human Readable
getByRole(BUTTON, "Login")

looks cleaner than:

//div[3]/button[2]
Real-Time Example

If developer changes:

<button class="btn-primary">Login</button>

to:

<button class="new-style">Login</button>

getByRole() still works.

CSS locator may fail.

Advanced SDET2 Answer

“Playwright recommends getByRole() because it provides stable, accessibility-aware, and user-centric locators. Since it relies on semantic roles and accessible 
names instead of implementation details like CSS classes or DOM structure, tests become more maintainable and less flaky.”
